response:
The selectivity factor \( s = k_e / k_m \) is a key parameter in determining the amplification of enantiomeric excess (e.e.) in a dimerization process. A higher selectivity factor \( s \) leads to greater amplification of the e.e. of the substrate. This is because a larger \( s \) means the reaction preferentially forms the homochiral dimer over the meso dimer, enriching the product mixture with the desired enantiomer. 

\[
\boxed{\text{The selectivity factor } s \text{ determines the extent of enantioselectivity in the dimerization process, with a higher } s \text{ leading to greater amplification of the e.e.}
}
\]

